@meeting train.m1.torres-zeke
// Pillar 5, M1 rounds (Day 2, Tuesday morning, the Works bay; tea is at 13:00 now). Head writer, seat W.
// Playtest 2 (item 1, head writer W2): Torres no longer walks the round with you. He explains it here, then the first
// round goes up on the job board; taking it, the goal arrow and green tips (round.tip.*) walk you round (seat E1, U).
title: Torres's round
from: Zeke Torres
gist: Zeke explained rounds: walk a route of machines, look at each one, mark fine, worn or something's off. Your first round is on the board.
== lines
torres-zeke: Got a minute? Let me tell you about rounds.   [gesture]
torres-zeke > you: Four or five machines on one deck. Look at each. Listen. Then mark it.
torres-zeke: Fine, worn, or something's off. That's all the clipboard wants.
torres-zeke: Worn's the one we're after. Catch it worn, it's a quick service. Miss it, it's a repair.
? choice
* What if I mark it wrong?   rel:torres-zeke+2 flavour:practical
  torres-zeke: Then nothing happens. Or I say "looks all right to me". Nobody gets told off.
* How do you know it's worn?   rel:torres-zeke+1 flavour:listen
  torres-zeke: You hear it. A rattle that wasn't there. Heat where there shouldn't be. You'll see.
* Sounds easy.   rel:torres-zeke+1 flavour:cheer
  torres-zeke: It is. That's the secret. Easy, every day.   [laugh]
torres-zeke: Your first round's on the board. Take it, and your device will show you the way.
torres-zeke: I'll be about if you get stuck. Off you go.
